#{[#edb/ref 17592186049304 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055590} :activity/id #uuid "6a57c066-8362-5ded-8fc6-fe55df2e5373" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055590 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055974 :tutorial/content "We add two matrices by adding the corresponding entries. We can only do this if they have the same number of rows and columns, that is, they have the same dimensions.\n\nFor example, consider the matrices\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe see that both $A$ and $B$ are $2\\times 2$ matrices, so we can add them. The sum ${A} + {B}\\,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+1 & 3+0 \\\\ 2+0 & 4+(-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, the sum of two matrices is another matrix with the same dimensions." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055591}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055591 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054648 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054545 :question/id #uuid "45832301-e578-578d-8866-147b998a1c22" :question/math-academy-id "e-4374" :question/problem "Calculate $A+B,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We add the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-10 & -5+2 \\\\ -2+2 & 3-4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Adding Square Matrices"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055594}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055592 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054649 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054546 :question/id #uuid "fc4a66f3-5180-5ac0-bd4d-830196fa459a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4375" :question/problem "Calculate $A-B,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We subtract the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-B & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-0 & -1-(-1) \\\\ -1-2 & 0-(-1) \\\\ 3-(-1) & 2-3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Subtracting Matrices"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055593 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055975 :tutorial/content "We can subtract matrices in the same way that we add matrices: we subtract the corresponding entries. Again, we can only do this if they have the same number of rows and columns, that is, they have the same dimensions.\n\nFor example, consider the matrices\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe see that both $A$ and $B$ are $2\\times 2$ matrices, so, we can subtract them. The difference ${A} - {B}\\,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-1 & 3-0 \\\\ 2-0 & 4-(-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, the difference of two matrices is another matrix with the same dimensions." :tutorial/title "Subtracting Matrices"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055592}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055594 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054650 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054547 :question/id #uuid "b77816bd-e128-5888-9973-97c51c99f7f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4396" :question/problem "Calculate ${A}+ {B},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We add the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+(-2) & 3+(-3) & 1+1 & 0+1 \\\\ 2+1 & 4+(-2) & -1+3 & 2+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Adding Non-Square Matrices"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055593}}] :activity/title "Adding and Subtracting Matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054891}] [#edb/ref 17592186050979 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060636} :activity/id #uuid "ab353e02-ddaa-5627-8691-ddb0bfb2272a" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060926 :tutorial/content "We can multiply matrices by numbers. This type of multiplication is known as **scalar multiplication**. Here, a **scalar** is just a number.\n\nFor example, consider the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo multiply ${A}$ by the scalar $2,$ we just multiply each entry of ${A}$ by $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A & =2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 3 \\\\ 2\\cdot 2 & 2\\cdot 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, to multiply a $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ by a scalar $c,$ we multiply each entry of $A$ by $c$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} & … & {a}_{1n} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22} & … & {a}_{2n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ {a}_{m1} & {a}_{m2} & … & {a}_{mn}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060637}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059740 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059549 :question/id #uuid "4391bd7a-560e-5b59-bf5c-b68ff235c05e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4380" :question/problem "Calculate $-2A$, if \n$A=\\left\\lbrack\n    \t\\matrix {\n        \t2 & -1 \\\n        \t4 & 7\n    \t}\n    \t\\right\\rbrack.$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each entry of $A$ by $-2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2A & =-2\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\cdot 2 & -2\\cdot (-1) \\\\ -2\\cdot 4 & -2\\cdot 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ -8 & -14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Matrix by a Scalar"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060638}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060638 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059741 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059550 :question/id #uuid "69ac9cdd-b27b-5b90-be25-02252df54e9e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4381" :question/problem "If $k {A} =\n\\left\\lbrack\n    \t\\matrix {\n        \t1 & 3 & 4 \\\n        \t6 & 0 & -2\n    \t}\n    \t\\right\\rbrack,$\nwhere ${A} =\n\\left\\lbrack\n    \t\\matrix {\n        \t2 & 6 & 8 \\\n        \t12 & 0 & -4\n    \t}\n    \t\\right\\rbrack$\nand $k$ is a scalar, calculate the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "To express $kA,$ we multiply each entry of $A$ by $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & 8 \\\\ 12 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2k & 6k & 8k \\\\ 12k & 0 & -4k\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the above result equal to the matrix given for $kA$ in the problem statement:\n\n$$\n\\left\\lbrack\n    \t\\matrix {\n        \t2k & 6k & 8k \\\n        \t12k & 0 & -4k\n    \t}\n    \t\\right\\rbrack\n=\n\\left\\lbrack\n    \t\\matrix {\n        \t1 & 3 & 4 \\\n        \t6 & 0 & -2\n    \t}\n    \t\\right\\rbrack\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by equating any two corresponding (nonzero) elements. Let's choose the top-left elements, $2k$ and $1.$ Equating these elements, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k & =1 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We would still get the same result if we chose any other two corresponding (nonzero) elements."} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Value of a Scalar"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060640}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060639 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059742 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059551 :question/id #uuid "1828e8ba-504d-5b20-b5fa-82005c7588f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-4382" :question/problem "Calculate $2 {A} + \\dfrac{1}{2} {B}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${B}=\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        0 & 2 \\\n        4 & 2\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack\n.$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each entry of $A$ by $2$ and each entry of $B$ by $\\dfrac{1}{2},$ and then add the resulting matrices, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A+\\frac{1}{2}B & =2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 5 \\\\ 2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4 & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+0 & 10+1 \\\\ 2+2 & 4+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 11 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Linear Combination of Two Matrices"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060640 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060927 :tutorial/content "Since scalar multiplication is done entry-wise, it obeys the same kind of rules as multiplication of real numbers.\n\nFor example, for any numbers $c_1, c_2$ and any matrix $A,$ we can perform scalar multiplication in any order: \n\n$$\nc_1 (c_2 A) = (c_1 c_2) A = c_2 (c_1 A)\n$$\n\nMoreover, we can distribute a matrix over the sum or difference of scalars:\n\n$$\n(c_1 \\pm c_2)A = c_1A \\pm c_2A\n$$\n\nWe can also distribute scalar multiplication over the sum or difference of two matrices:\n\n$$\nc (A \\pm B) = c A \\pm c B\n$$" :tutorial/title "Properties of Scalar Multiplication of Matrices"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060639}}] :activity/title "Scalar Multiplication of Matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059880}] [#edb/ref 17592186048863 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101859} :activity/id #uuid "d0d06916-b04a-5057-b794-744d1e7a0e9f" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101859 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101954 :tutorial/content "If we want to add (or subtract) three or more matrices, all operations should be done elementwise. For example, suppose\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen we can calculate ${A}+{B}-{C}$ by performing the operations on each corresponding entry, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B-C & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+(-1)-0 \\\\ 1+2-5 \\\\ -1+0-(-2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101860}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101860 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100991 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100686 :question/id #uuid "0928c2fa-a772-5d39-a638-1b1d0e71be2f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4378" :question/problem "Calculate $A-(B-C),$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Performing the operations on each corresponding entry, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(B-C) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1-0 & -1-(-5) \\\\ 2-1 & 0-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1-(-1) & 0-4 \\\\ 2-1 & -2-(-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum or Difference of Three or More Matrices"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101861}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101861 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101955 :tutorial/content "Since addition and subtraction of matrices are performed elementwise, they have the same properties as addition and subtraction of real numbers.\n\nFor example, if $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are three $m \\times n$ matrices then we can add them in any order:\n\n$$\n(A+B)+C = A+(B+C) = A+B+C\n$$\n\nMoreover, we can distribute a subtraction over matrices in parentheses:\n\n$$\nA - (B + C) = A - B - C\n$$" :tutorial/title "Properties of Matrix Addition and Subtraction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101862}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101862 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100992 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100687 :question/id #uuid "1b15db42-aba2-575c-a01e-b715450329fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-4379" :question/problem "Find $A+C-(B+A),$ if\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the expression by distributing the subtraction over the matrices in the parentheses: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+C-(B+A) & =A+C-B-A \\\\  & =\\cancel{A}+C-B-\\cancel{A} \\\\  & =C-B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left with a single subtraction. So, we subtract the corresponding entries of the two matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-B & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3-3 & -1-(-1) \\\\ 1-2 & 2-0 \\\\ -6-(-4) & 1-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum or Difference of Three or More Matrices Using Distributivity"}}] :activity/title "Properties of Matrix Addition" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101035}] [#edb/ref 17592186050714 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111735} :activity/id #uuid "ec05fcce-5a67-533c-840a-322806556dbc" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111735 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111872 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $P$ is a point in the first quadrant that lies on the unit circle. We can then draw a right triangle, where the hypotenuse is the line segment between the origin and the point. \n\n![](images/4b/4bae3a49b5557df9e5b50280d744aa72048b2d224df042e103a6b5d156467de7.png)\n\nSince the hypotenuse is the radius of the unit circle, its length must be equal to $1.$ And, since the triangle is a right triangle, the $(x,y)$ coordinates of any point on the unit circle in the first quadrant must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=1.\n$$\n\nWe can use this to find the $y$-coordinate of a point given the $x$-coordinate, and vice-versa.\n\nFor example, suppose that the $x$-coordinate of a point $P$ in the first quadrant on the unit circle is $\\dfrac{3}{7}.$ To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=\\dfrac{3}{7}$ into the above and solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tx^2+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t          \\left(\\dfrac{3}{7}\\right)^2+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{9}{49}+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\ty^2&= 1-\\dfrac{9}{49}\\[5pt]\n\t\ty^2&= \\dfrac{40}{49}\\[5pt]\n\t\ty&= \\pm\\sqrt{\\dfrac{40}{49}}\\[5pt]\n\t        y &= \\pm \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}\n\n}\n$$\n\nSince $P$ lies in the first quadrant, $y$ must be positive. So we select the positive answer. \n\nTherefore, our final answer is $y=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}.$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111736}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111736 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110754 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110456 :question/id #uuid "42313322-7535-5969-ad84-958449e48c1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3480" :question/problem "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $y$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\dfrac{5}{9}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=1 .\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=\\dfrac{5}{9}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tx^2+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t        x^2+\\left(\\dfrac{5}{9}\\right)^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\tx^2+\\left(\\dfrac{25}{81}\\right)&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\tx^2&= 1-\\dfrac{25}{81}\\[5pt]\n\t\tx^2&= \\dfrac{56}{81}\\[5pt]\n\t\tx&= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{56}{81}}\\[5pt]\n\t        x&= \\pm \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.\n}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $x$ must be positive. So, we select the positive answer. \n\nTherefore, our final answer is $x=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Using the Pythagorean Theorem on the Unit Circle"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111737}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111737 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111867 :tutorial/content "Recall that the coordinates of a point on the unit circle with a central angle $\\theta$ have the property\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf this point is in the first quadrant, we can substitute these values into the Pythagorean Identity from before to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}\\theta +{sin}^{2}\\theta  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last expression is known as the **Pythagorean trigonometric identity,** and is arguably the most important equation in trigonometry.\n\nThe Pythagorean trigonometric identity holds for *every* angle $\\theta,$ not just if $\\theta$ is in the first quadrant. In this lesson, we're focusing on the first quadrant only. We'll learn how to deal with angles in other quadrants in a future lesson." :tutorial/title "The Pythagorean Identity"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111738}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111738 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110755 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110457 :question/id #uuid "02c747c2-0eda-5c53-8e43-829f878dd278" :question/math-academy-id "e-3482" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aad0b38f5afb88c13039ac577a927e232d84905de40297ed824c966795ce7bb4.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{2}{3},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta.$" :question/worked-solution "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\dfrac{2}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. \n\nSo our final answer is $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 5}{3}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111739}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111739 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110756 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110458 :question/id #uuid "bbf71023-510c-54af-9d0c-4df6744e3007" :question/math-academy-id "e-3484" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e50848432dd18a4a5dc64d4e5a5cfb8b3e14e9fed6231774dd83ead5552cd463.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\csc\\theta.$" :question/worked-solution "Again, any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{24}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{5}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{5}{7}\\right)} = \\dfrac{7}{5}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111741}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111741 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110758 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110459 :question/id #uuid "ba6cc6a0-dbb8-58d2-9d02-c76310028d4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3485" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e666ddc8a8eda452abd833e963446c96de5d34dfbf76e9c92564e85a48080524.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{4}{5},$ what is $\\tan\\theta?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=\\dfrac{4}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{4}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. Therefore $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{3}{5}.$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta},\n$$\n\nwe finally get\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)} = \\dfrac {3}{4}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}}] :activity/title "Using the Pythagorean Identity in the First Quadrant" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110825}]}
